/*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  ChemicalPlantTutorial / MAIN / system/flowsheetDict   (SECTOR -- COMPOSITE)

  THE MAIN SECTOR: the plant-level operations that belong to no specialised
  sector.  Here that is one unit, the JuiceSplitter, which takes the single
  RawJuice header and splits it 2/3 to CONCENTRATION and 1/3 to FERMENTATION.

  WHY A LEVEL FOR ONE UNIT (2026-09-05, amended 2026-09-07).  Until 09-05 the
  JuiceSplitter sat at the plant root beside the sectors -- a PascalCase unit
  folder among CAPS folders, breaking the convention this case's own header
  declares.  The rule the plant now teaches: a folder in CAPS is always a
  level of the plant's geography, and a plant-level unit lives in MAIN/.
  MAIN/ is THE DOMAIN'S OWN LEVEL, not a sector like the others (the
  2026-09-07 amendment), which is why the plant's own inlets and every stream
  crossing two sectors live there too -- a stream's file goes at the lowest
  level whose subtree contains every endpoint of it.  The STATE views repeat
  the whole geography: 0/ and converged/ both carry MAIN, CONCENTRATION,
  DRYING, FERMENTATION.  A DERIVATIVE view carries only what it has something
  to say about -- design/ has no MAIN/, because a splitter realises no sized
  item -- and that absence is a fact, not a gap to fill.  Flat cases are
  untouched: a case with no sectors has no MAIN either (its units ARE the
  plant).
\*-----------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

sectors    ( JuiceSplitter );

boundary
{
    inlets   ( RawJuice );
    outlets  ( ToConcentration  ToFermentation );
}

connections
{
    RawJuice         { to JuiceSplitter/In; }
    ToConcentration  { from JuiceSplitter/ToConcentration; }
    ToFermentation   { from JuiceSplitter/ToFermentation; }
}
